                            <p></p><h3>Definition of "model-2700":</h3><p><b>Model 2700</b> is the name of the original <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/rectangular-diaphragm">rectangular condenser capsule</a> design from Sweden&#8217;s <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/Pearl/DS-60">Pearl Microphone Lab</a>. When PML split into two companies in 1978, rights to the Model 2700 transferred to the new company, Milab. The Model 2700 capsule is still made by Milab today.</p><p><img src="/images/mic_extras/milab/model_2700_capsule.jpg" width="100" class="thumb shaft" alt="Milab Model 2700 capsule" />The capsule is a dual-diaphragm, dual-backplate design with outside dimensions 36mm x 19mm. Both membranes are thin polyester (Mylar), metallized with a layer of vaporized aluminum that is reportedly just a few atoms thick.</p><p>Aluminum is a lighter metal than gold, and is used here to improve the capsule&#8217;s transient response.</p><p>The rectangular capsule design is said to be superior to traditional round designs for the following reasons:</p><ul><li>The narrow dimension of the capsule allows the microphone body and grille to be smaller than would a round-capsule large-diaphragm microphone.</li><li>The rectangular capsule does not produce the high-amplitude resonance peak in the midrange that all round large-diaphragm capsules do; peaks are lower in amplitude and higher in frequency, where they have less effect on the microphone&#8217;s sound.</li><li>The increased directionality of the rectangular capsule in the vertical plane allows for better rejection of unwanted sounds (assuming it is positioned advantageously).</li></ul><p>Read more about the <a href="http://www.milabmic.com/content.asp?page=capsule">Milab Model 2700 capsule</a>.</p><p>See also the <a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/model-2900">Model 2900</a> capsule, which is identical in size to the 2700, but higher in sensitivity and lower in self-noise.</p><p>(This page formerly described the Model 2700 as a new, very long capsule developed in the 2000s by PML: a 75mm x 15mm capsule used in the ELM microphone series. This model is more commonly referred to as the &#8220;<a href="http://recordinghacks.com/microphones/tag/elm-capsule">ELM capsule</a>,&#8221; even within Pearl literature.)</p>
